#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046645} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547157} :activity/id #uuid "49557175-272f-5590-8a63-95f82b2b3ff6" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050536 :topic/id #uuid "51d6809f-cbef-5f23-be22-3c2d79930c37" :topic/math-academy-id 4218} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547093 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546982 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d2c19332-1daa-53e4-8c7e-f6edf5736887" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546716}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546716} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9413410-1a9d-50b7-9182-e546f0368ec5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546910}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2a9dfae3-b740-55e6-abd9-d9909c3ad491" :question/math-academy-id "q-344835" :question/problem "![](images/48/48b2ec3916610f624aa308d9c395c43eb7a34418a6457c514dc40ecc82bf2f99.png)\n\nFind the least distance $d$ between the curve $y=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}+1$ and the point $(2,1).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$d=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(2,1)$ and a general point $P(x,y)$:\n\n$$\nS=(x-2)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}+1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =(x-2)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-2)^{2}+(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}+1-1)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-2)^{2}+(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1})^{2} \\\\  & =(x-2)^{2}+{x}^{2}+1 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-4x+4+{x}^{2}+1 \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}-4x+5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=4x-4 & =0 \\\\ 4x & =4 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(x)=4>0,$ so $x=1$ gives a minimum of $S.$\n\nSince $S$ gives the squared distance between the two points, the actual distance $d$ between the two points when $x=1$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{S(1)}.\n$$\n\nComputing this quantity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{S(1)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2(1)^{2}-4(1)+5} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "14d7851b-2592-5b3a-96a9-8d51149c8a93" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547133}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547098 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547006 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54db5f0b-c4e1-5e3f-bd51-fea3d81ff682" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546437}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546437} :answer-field/id #uuid "d77e5f19-97fb-5309-b828-6d59517070d0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546936}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5957844f-5ee9-5592-b52f-3fea8df780b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-315639" :question/problem "![](images/83/835ffbb783651cfc8f6210fe46f792d7c68f41926a4775e56a570e9bb87b7462.png)\n\nFind the $x$-coordinate of the point on the curve $y=\\sqrt{9x+1}$ that is closest to the point $(8,0).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(8,0)$ and a general point $P(x,y)$:\n\n$$\nS=(x-8)^{2}+{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\sqrt{9x+1}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =(x-8)^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\  & =(x-8)^{2}+(\\sqrt{9x+1})^{2} \\\\  & =(x-8)^{2}+9x+1 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-16x+64+9x+1 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-7x+65.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x-7 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =7 \\\\ x & =\\frac{7}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(x)=2>0.$ Therefore, $x=\\frac{7}{2}$ is a minimum of $S.$\n\nSo, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) is minimized when $x=\\frac{7}{2}.$"} :step/id #uuid "24b7c382-5fad-5c82-8a7d-642129b1c47a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547093}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547102 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547033 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a4c3d4e-5b73-5c34-a4c3-53c48c541b63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546376} {:answer/id #uuid "72775d93-ea9b-54c6-9567-4c8e764952f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{19}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546504} {:answer/id #uuid "b641d9de-cddd-5173-aac6-c811671c0ffd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{19}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546659} {:answer/id #uuid "bb82afd5-4e30-5905-aad5-28701a369b25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546672} {:answer/id #uuid "c96541f4-c7b4-562c-ab5d-50aefc222234" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546699}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546699} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0295622-35a4-5953-aa14-52d047c70fc3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546938}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "907519ae-3321-5175-a88f-6dce32f2cb03" :question/math-academy-id "q-18500" :question/problem "Find the $y$-coordinate of the point on the curve $y={x}^{2}-2x$ that is closest to the point $(1,4).$" :question/worked-solution "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(1,4)$ and a general point $P(x,y)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-1)^{2}+(y-4)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-2x+1+{y}^{2}-8y+16 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-2x+{y}^{2}-8y+17\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y={x}^{2}-2x.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(y) & =y+{y}^{2}-8y+17 \\\\  & ={y}^{2}-7y+17.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(y)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(y)=2y-7 & =0 \\\\ 2y & =7 \\\\ y & =\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(y)=2>0.$ Therefore, $y=\\frac{7}{2}$ is a minimum of $S.$\n\nSo, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) is minimized when $y=\\frac{7}{2}.$"} :step/id #uuid "2c5a3b83-ee27-5938-906f-66932bc34a39" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547098}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547133 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547054 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dbfe3030-34aa-5743-844c-793f65847a3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546736}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546736} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae0782a8-a8ce-5695-a710-1fed57c8a4e2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546916}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ceab1153-db5f-5530-aee8-9a4937774de9" :question/math-academy-id "q-344849" :question/problem "Find the least distance between the curve $y=\\sqrt{|x-1|}+2$ and the point $P(2,2).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$d=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(2,2)$ and a general point $P(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-2)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\sqrt{|x-1|}+2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-2)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-2)^{2}+{(\\sqrt{|x-1|}+2-2)}^{2} \\\\  & =(x-2)^{2}+|x-1|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x\\ge 1,$ we have $|x-1|=x-1,$ so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-2)^{2}+x-1 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-4x+4+x-1 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-3x+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x-3 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =3 \\\\ x & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(x)=2>0.$ Therefore, $x=\\frac{3}{2}$ is a minimum of $S.$\n\nSince $S$ gives the squared distance between the two points, the actual distance $d$ between the two points when $x=\\frac{3}{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{S\\,(\\frac{3}{2})}.\n$$\n\nComputing this quantity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{S\\,(\\frac{3}{2})} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-3(\\frac{3}{2})+3} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{4}-\\frac{9}{2}+3} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a088826c-f52e-5e69-8491-0696adb96bbc"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547157 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198944 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8aef2d58-4f97-565f-92e2-f7a18012072e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197535} {:answer/id #uuid "9859e4bd-f0ea-5c7d-9135-59281613b137" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197536} {:answer/id #uuid "9cfb6463-e116-5658-af37-8050c0e1de81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197537} {:answer/id #uuid "bfdbe21a-4020-5375-bc77-32e9f4bfc13b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197538} {:answer/id #uuid "567ac564-b878-51eb-8aa3-e254ea745d62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197539}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186197535} :answer-field/id #uuid "e42b1007-56d3-5b64-871d-19d6670e6fc2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197534}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f5ed0b17-cac6-5f50-8a04-2449a141ebc0" :question/math-academy-id "q-3442" :question/problem "![](images/72/720713e7e1dbfbc9ce3ebb486e8744327c633745d00df7dc862d794eefe57bbc.png)\n\nWhat is the least distance between the curve $y=\\sqrt{x}$ and the point $(4,0)?$" :question/worked-solution "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(4,0)$ and a general point $P(x,y)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-4)^{2}+(y-0)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\sqrt{x}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-4)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-4)^{2}+(\\sqrt{x})^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-8x+16+x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-7x+16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x-7 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =7 \\\\ x & =\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(x)=2>0.$ Therefore, $x=\\frac{7}{2}$ is a minimum of $S.$\n\nSince $S$ gives the squared distance between the two points, the actual distance $d$ between the two points when $x=\\frac{7}{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{S(\\frac{7}{2})}.\n$$\n\nComputing this quantity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{S(\\frac{7}{2})} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-7(\\frac{7}{2})+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{15}{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "db305256-c310-5e02-ade5-477388d7db01" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547102}}] :activity/title "Optimizing Distances to Curves" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546240}]}
